A||||2||fiero87||Major Tune up help!||||5||
Z||000000||fiero87||11-09-2000||04:20 PM||||Hello,<BR>I am planning a MAJOR tune up for my newly aquired 86 GT.  I was planning on replacing almost all sensors, filters, etc i could physically reach.  Right now the car is running very poorly with below avg performance.  Hopefully with this and some head work it will run like a brand new fiero.  What i need help with is a list of different things i should replace.  This car has been sitting for sometime, as i undestand, so i don't want to leave anything out.  Also, I have some major lifter noise so i was planning on having them replaced.  While i'm at it, should i look into having my valve guide seals and rocker springs replaced as well??  How involved is this work, (i'm only minorly mechanicly inclined with a lot of friends who know much more)  should i have this done professionally?  Any info all of you experts can provide would be a great help.  Thanks, This is an invaluable place for information.  <BR>Tim||||reg||5
Z||000001||2xsess||11-09-2000||05:27 PM||||First change the obvious stuff, plugs,wires,cap,rotor, fuel and air filter,pcv,O2 sensor, thermostat.Depending on how long the car has been sitting you might want to put a new cap on the thermostat housing and the radiator. Of course this all depending on how much $$ you want to dump, some of these might still be in good working condition.<P>If you're changing the lifters you might as well have the valve seals done. kind of like preventative maintenance. <P>I'm sure there will be may more suggestions coming soon.<BR>Good Luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||11-09-2000||07:40 PM||||Fiero87 looks like you are taking the replace everything wrought, which is more expensive but easy. If I were you I would not remove the heads and I would not replace the springs unless the engine was overheated a couple of times or it has high mileage. Here is a test you can perform to test your springs but you should give your fiero a good tun up before you do this so that the readings are accurate. <P>Get a vacuum gauge and hook it up to manifold vacuum somewhere on the plenum close to the throttle body. With the vacuum gauge hooked up rev the engine steadily up to or close to the engines operating limits (5500 rpm). If the vacuum gauge needle is steady and vacuum increases steadily than your springs are good. If the needle starts bouncing even a little than you may have worm springs. When the needle bounces this means that the valves are bouncing off there seat (floating) and the springs are not doing their job. If the gauge has a rhythmic movement where the needle is not steady than you may have a sticking valve or leaking valve. <P>A finger placed on the valve spring retainer while the engine idles will feel the shock each time the loose valve hits its seat. Valve train noise occurs at half the speed of engine rpm. This is because the cam only turns once for every two turns of the crank. Lifters that stick because of varnish buildup can sometimes be loosened by squirting carburetor cleaner down the hollow push rod lubricating channel, where it contacts the rocker arm. Or you can try additives or just change your oil more frequently and this sometimes clears the engine of varnish. <P>Lifter noise at all engine speeds can be due to, dirt or varnish buildup, worn parts such as worn rocker arms or a cam lobe that is going flat, insufficient oil supply, oil too thin, and oil pressure too low.<P>Use an accurate oil pressure gauge to read the oil pressure. <P>You can change the valve guide seals without removing the heads and I would do this because they usually go early on GM engines and start the clog up the valve openings with carbon from the leaking oil. When they get really bad your engine will omit a small puff of blue smoke when you start it up for the first time in the morning.<P>I would replace the coolant temperature gauge, air intake temperature gauge, the temperature switch for the cold start injector, and throttle position sensor. Make sure you use dielectric grease for the spark plug boots when you service your ignition system and don’t go cheap on the tune up parts such as filter, rotor and cap, and wires. I would only use Genuine GM parts for that stuff. I know because I have used them all and there is a difference.<P>That’s all I can think of right now because I am too tired.  <BR>||||reg||1
